'''''Lampranthus coccineus''''', commonly known as the '''rooi vygie''', '''rose vygie''', or '''red brightfig''', is a flowering plant that blooms in spring and is endemic to the [[Western Cape]], where it forms part of the [[fynbos]] vegetation.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Lampranthus coccineus (Haw.) N.E.Br.|url=http://redlist.sanbi.org/species.php?species=88-39|work=REDLIST Sanbi}}</ref> It is found in the area stretching from [[Graafwater]] to [[Saldanha, South Africa|Saldanha]] and [[Darling, South Africa|Darling]], with an extent of occurrence of 1,063 km². However, the species has already lost 80% of its habitat to crop cultivation, and only five small subpopulations remain. Crops grown in the area include rooibos tea and potatoes. The total population consists of fewer than 250 plants.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Lampranthus coccineus (Haw.) N.E.Br.|url=https://powo.science.kew.org/taxon/urn:lsid:ipni.org:names:362183-1|work=Plants of the World Online}}</ref>

It is a fast-growing, trailing plant with small, fleshy leaves and glossy, bright red flowers that open only on sunny days. There are many other related species. The plant prefers full sun and little water.

